<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of Venezuela</h1><p class="st-meta">1528 CE – 1777 CE · capital Caracas</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Spanish governorate of the Caribbean coast of South America, centred first on Coro and then Caracas, which grew rich on cacao.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coro-venezuela">Coro</a> in 1527, and from 1528 the province was leased to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welser-family">Welser</a> bankers as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/klein-venedig">Klein-Venedig</a>. After the Welser lease ended in the 1540s, Spanish governors took over. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-losada">Diego de Losada</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Santiago de León de Caracas</a> in 1567, and it became the capital in 1578.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The province was governed by a governor and captain-general under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-general-of-santo-domingo">Audiencia of Santo Domingo</a> and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Santa Fe de Bogotá</a>. Its economy grew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> plantations worked by enslaved Africans, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> cacao was exported to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. From 1728 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guipuzcoan-company-of-caracas">Guipuzcoan Company of Caracas</a> held a trade monopoly, provoking the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-francisco-de-leon">Juan Francisco de León</a> in 1749. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> elite, known as mantuanos, dominated society.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1777 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III</a> united the province with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumana">Cumaná</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayana-province">Guayana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maracaibo">Maracaibo</a> and the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margarita-island">Margarita</a> to form the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-general-of-venezuela">Captaincy General of Venezuela</a>. The province's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> elite later led the independence movement of 1810.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-venezuela.jpg" alt="Province of Venezuela" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Mathew Carey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_venezuela(1760-1839).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1528 CE – 1777 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Caracas</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Diego de Losada, Juan de Pimentel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Governorate of Venezuela</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1652&amp;focus=province-of-venezuela" aria-label="Province of Venezuela on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2836 1786 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Province of Venezuela lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3156 2042L3083 2009M3156 2042L4910 989M3156 2042L3156 2042"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3083 2009h0M4910 989h0M3156 2042h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3156 2042h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3156 2042h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Caracas. <a href="/atlas?year=1652&amp;focus=province-of-venezuela">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
